{
  "subtype": "faceted_decomposition",
  "concept": "hardware_hiccups",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "hardware_component",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "The affected component (printer, monitor, keyboard, etc.) is a genuine constitutive aspect OF a hardware issue -- the 'what part of the hardware' facet. It carves the concept along a valid dimension."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_type",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "The nature of the malfunction (connection issue, display issue, overheating, power/boot) is an aspect OF the hardware hiccup -- the 'what kind of failure' facet. Note some values ('Hardware Issue') are near-restatements and 'Display Issue' overlaps with the Monitor component, weakening exclusivity."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_critical_hardware",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "external_predictor",
      "rationale": "A criticality/severity flag is an assessment attribute or predictor of impact, not a constitutive part of what the hardware hiccup IS. It describes importance, not an aspect of the issue itself."
    }
  ],
  "facet_columns": ["hardware_component", "issue_type"],
  "non_facet_columns": ["is_critical_hardware"],
  "mutual_exclusivity": 0.55,
  "exhaustiveness": 0.5,
  "rationale": "Two of three columns are genuine facets of a hardware hiccup: the affected component and the type/nature of failure. These are legitimate MECE-style dimensions, but mutual exclusivity is only moderate because failure-type values partly recapitulate component identity (e.g., 'Display Issue' <-> Monitor, 'Color/Print Issue' <-> Printer) and 'Hardware Issue' is a restatement of the whole. Exhaustiveness is moderate: component and failure-mode are covered, but other constitutive aspects (e.g., symptom manifestation, when-in-lifecycle it occurs, resolution type) are absent. The third column, is_critical_hardware, is a severity/importance predictor rather than a constitutive part-of facet, so it fails the meronymy test."
}

